## Solution Idea

Let
- $a$ = number of $-1$'s in $P$, $b$ = number of $-1$'s in $Q$,
- $A$ = multiset of fixed values of $P$ ($n_A=N-a$), $B$ = multiset of fixed values of $Q$ ($n_B=N-b$).

After fixing the common sum $S$, each of the $N$ pairs is one of four types:
- fixed $p$ with fixed $q$: needs $p+q=S$;
- fixed $p$ with a free $Q$ slot: needs $S\ge p$;
- fixed $q$ with a free $P$ slot: needs $S\ge q$;
- free with free: always fine ($0$ and $S$).

So **every fixed value must be $\le S$**, i.e. $S\ge \max(A\cup B)$, and if $M(S)$ is the largest number of fixed–fixed pairs we can form,
$$M(S)=\sum_{v}\min\big(\mathrm{cnt}_A(v),\mathrm{cnt}_B(S-v)\big),$$
we need the leftovers to fit into free slots: $n_A-M\le b$ and $n_B-M\le a$. Both conditions are the same:
$$M(S)\ \ge\ K:=N-a-b .$$
(One also checks the counts of free–free pairs match: $a-(n_B-M)=b-(n_A-M)=a+b-N+M\ge 0$, same condition.)

**Case $a+b\ge N$:** $K\le 0$, take $S=\max$ fixed value → answer `Yes`.

**Case $K\ge 1$:** some fixed–fixed pair exists, so $S=v+w$ for some value $v\in A,\ w\in B$. Deduplicate $A$ and $B$ (at most $2000$ distinct values each) and enumerate all $\le 4\cdot10^6$ pairs $(v,w)$: the pair contributes exactly $\min(\mathrm{cnt}_A(v),\mathrm{cnt}_B(w))$ to $M(v+w)$. Aggregate by sum (sort packed keys) and test whether some $S\ge\max$ fixed value has $M(S)\ge K$.

